# Examples that carry a refusal, and how many.
#
# `nts check` **exits 0 when an exported function refuses.** It compiles what it
# can, runs that, compares it with node and reports agreement over the functions
# that survived. So an example can lose most of its cases to a refusal and still
# be counted by `backend_examples` as agreeing -- which is what happened while
# `examples/in-on-a-native-receiver` was being written: with the change under
# test disabled, seven of its nine functions refused, `nts check` said "checked
# 58 cases across 2 function(s), agreed on every case", and exited 0.
#
# A fixture that cannot fail is worth nothing, and the floor above it says 147
# either way. This file is what makes the difference visible: every example that
# refuses anything is listed with its count and the reason, and the step fails on
# an example that is not here or that refuses more than it says.
#
# A count that has gone *down* is progress and prints a note rather than failing;
# edit the number when that happens. A count that has gone up, or a name that is
# not here, is a fixture quietly measuring less than it says it does.
#
# Format: name<space>count<space>why. Blank lines and `#` are ignored.
#
# The count is `nts hir`'s, which is **not** `nts check`'s: `check` prints the
# cascades below a refusal as well, so it says three for `calls` where the
# lowering refuses one thing and two functions fall over behind it. Counting the
# root is what makes the number stable when a cascade lengthens.

# Deliberate: these exist to hold constructs that refuse, and the count is the
# point rather than a debt.
generic-classes-unsupported 8 the constructs the name promises
generator-unsupported 3 the constructs the name promises
dates-unsupported 6 the constructs the name promises
async-unsupported 4 the constructs the name promises
# Was 2 until `86994fb4` landed the mapped-callback arm, which is one of the
# two constructs this example's name promises. The remaining one is a
# `for...of` over an object type.
#
# **Stale from that commit until 2026-09-13, and it printed every run.** The
# step reports a fallen count as progress and passes, deliberately -- a gate
# that goes red when you fix something teaches people to stop fixing things --
# so the only thing standing between the note and the edit is somebody acting
# on a line in a *passing* run. Nobody did, including on the run where this
# same file was audited row by row: that audit compared the third column and
# the second was the one that had moved.
#
# The cost is not tidiness. The comparison is `count > want`, so a `want` left
# at 2 lets a regression back to 2 pass silently -- a blind spot exactly the
# size of the progress, over the code that just changed.
array-from-unsupported 1 the constructs the name promises
mathops-unsupported 1 the constructs the name promises
# One of four arms. The other three are the shapes this refusal must *not*
# take, so a count of 2 here means the refusal has grown a case it should not
# have -- which it did twice while being written.
a-throw-that-stays-in-its-function 1 the constructs the name promises

# Incidental: an example written for something else that happens to contain a
# construct the compiler refuses. Each is a real gap and each is somebody's next
# item; the number is what stops a second one arriving unnoticed.
# Two exported generics nothing in the file instantiates. They were never
# compiled and the export could never have been filled; what changed on
# 2026-09-11 is that the compiler says so instead of emitting nothing and
# leaving the napi wrapper to report the effect with the cause unsaid.
advanced 2 an exported generic function this program never instantiates
library 1 a parameter of intersection type
module-evaluation 1 a regular expression literal
calls 1 an erased value where a concrete representation is wanted
jsx 2 a jsx self closing element
enum-reverse-map-unsupported 2 the constructs the name promises
